course: dictionary
lesson: 3340
title: The word ew
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Ew» is a small word, but it says a lot. It is an interjection, a sound people make to show disgust when something seems dirty, smelly or gross. You can often feel what it means from the sound alone.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Ew, gross!» after a slimy bug, or «Eww, that's disgusting» about a smelly bin, shows what the speaker thinks. Ew is informal, so you will find it in chat, comics and speech more than in formal writing.
explain: The more w's, the stronger the feeling: «ewwww» means the speaker is very grossed out. Context also shows when ew is used unkindly. When Bella says «Ew, why are you following me?» she is putting someone down, not just reacting to something dirty.
explain: The letters e and w together make the /oo/ sound, as in «new». Spelling can be tricky, so learn words like this one by heart.
fact: Ew is an interjection, a word that shows a feeling and often stands on its own.
fact: «Ew» means a sound people make to show disgust.
fact: Ew is informal, so it suits chat, comics and speech more than formal writing.
fact: In writing, more w's make the feeling stronger: «ewwww» is more disgusted than «ew».
fact: Ew can also be used unkindly to make someone feel small, so think about who is saying it and why.
exercise: «Eww, that's disgusting», said Ruby when she saw the slimy worm. What does ew show? | *Disgust at something gross | Great delight at a new toy | Hunger for lunch | Sleepiness after a long day
exercise: Ethan-Jay's mates say «Ew, Marmite?» about his food. What do they feel? | They want a second helping | They are very pleased to see him | They have just won a race | *They think the food looks gross
exercise: Which use of ew is unkind, not about something dirty? | Ethan-Jay eating Marmite | *Bella saying «Ew, why are you following me?» to Chloe | Grandad smelling burnt toast | Ruby holding a slimy worm
test: In «ewwwww», what do the extra w's show? | A word for a bird | *A stronger feeling of disgust | A quieter voice | A longer, more polite word
test: Could you use ew in a formal letter to the head teacher? | Yes, in a science report | Yes, always, because it is short | *No, it is informal, so keep it for chat and stories | Yes, if you write it in capitals
exam: Which best explains what «Ew, gross!» means? | It is a greeting, like hello | It is the name of a food | It means the speaker is very happy | *It is a sound that shows something seems dirty, smelly or gross
